      "source": [
        "### What this tells me\n",
        "\n",
        "**Random order** is the floor. Any rule that can't beat it is useless, and it gives a feel for how hard the problem is: with around 30 hotels per search, guessing gets the booked hotel into the top 5 only now and then.\n",
        "\n",
        "**Expedia's own order** looks unbeatable in the searches where Expedia chose the order, but that's partly an illusion. In those searches, the hotels Expedia put on top also got the extra clicks and bookings that come with sitting on top, as Part 1 showed. So Expedia's order is being marked on an exam it partly wrote itself.\n",
        "\n",
        "The random searches show this nicely. There the \"order\" is just the shuffle, and it knows nothing about the hotels. Yet sorting by it still scores above a fresh random guess, simply because guests clicked whatever happened to land on top. That is position bias showing up inside the score itself, and it's the clearest warning in this notebook: **any score that follows the order the guest saw will look better than it really is.**\n",
        "\n",
        "So the fairest comparison in this table is the other rules on the **random searches**. None of them knows the shuffled order, so none of them gets help from position.\n",
        "\n",
        "Among the simple rules, popularity built from past bookings and location score are the ones to watch. Cheapest first and most stars first sound sensible but on their own they mostly miss, which fits what any hotelier knows: people don't simply buy the cheapest or the fanciest room, they trade price against quality and location. That trade off is exactly what a model can learn and a single rule can't."
      ]
    },
